      "problem": "If ${y}^{2}+63=0,$ then $y=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+63 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\sqrt{-63}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $y$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\sqrt{-63} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{63\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{63}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
